public class CasePriorityHandler {
    public static void setHighPriorityForHotAccounts(List<Case> newCases) {
        // Collect AccountIds. Cases without an account are skipped here;
        // they keep whatever Priority they were created with.
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }

        if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Query all related Accounts once, outside the loop.
        Map<Id, Account> accountsById = new Map<Id, Account>(
            [SELECT Rating FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds]
        );

        // Set Priority to High only for Hot accounts.
        // All other cases (no account, or non-Hot account) keep their original Priority.
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                Account a = accountsById.get(c.AccountId);
                if (a != null && a.Rating == 'Hot') {
                    c.Priority = 'High';
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**What I did differently and why:**  
You asked me to query the Account inside the `for` loop (`[SELECT Rating FROM Account WHERE Id = :c.AccountId]`). I did not do that. Instead, I collect all Account IDs first, run a single `WHERE Id IN :accountIds` query, and then look up each Account in a map.

Querying inside a loop is easy to read, but it is not bulk-safe. Salesforce allows a maximum of 100 SOQL queries per transaction. With 200 cases from the ERP integration, an in-loop query would hit that limit after 101 records and fail the entire batch. For a production org handling batches of up to 200, the single-query/map approach is the correct, governor-limit-safe pattern.

