    /**
     * Applies the standard product discount to new opportunity line items that
     * do not already have one.
     *
     * - Discount == null: the rep did not type a discount, so set the standard
     *   discount from PricingRules.
     * - Discount == 0 or any other value: an explicit rep entry; keep it as-is.
     *
     * Discount logic stays in PricingRules; this handler only calls it and
     * writes the result onto the record in a before-insert context (no DML,
     * no recursion risk).
     *
     * @param newLineItems Trigger.new from the OpportunityLineItem trigger
     */
    public static void applyStandardDiscounts(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLineItems) {
        if (newLineItems == null || newLineItems.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLineItems) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                line.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## What I'd change and why

- **Bulkify `PricingRules` (the blocking issue at 200 lines).** `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(Id)` runs `SELECT Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id = :productId` on every call. Calling it once per line in a before-insert trigger means 200 lines with distinct products would exceed the 100 SOQL queries per transaction limit. I can't modify `PricingRules` per the task, so I'd ask the pricing team to add a bulk method such as `Map<Id, Decimal> standardDiscountsFor(Set<Id> productIds)` that queries `Product2` once and caches family discounts, then call it once in the handler and apply the map. Until then, this handler works only for smaller batches.
